 "final_text": "All three fields recorded. Final answer to Sam:\n\n**Total exposure to Adventure Works: $113,799.19**\n\n- **ERP open balance:** $94,749.19 \u2014 3 open invoices on Adventure Works Cycles in the main ERP (SYNCUS-0002), cross-verified two independent ways. (There are 35 look-alike \"Adventure Works <Region>\" accounts in the ERP that are decoys/unrelated companies, not real subsidiaries \u2014 excluded; including them would have inflated this ~9x.)\n- **Subsidiary net balance:** $19,050.00 \u2014 CES Direct LLC (a subsidiary of the same parent) bills Adventure Works separately in its own Books system: $22,200 gross open invoices less a $3,150 unapplied credit memo that the standard reports don't net out.\n- **Combined exposure:** $113,799.19 \u2014 these are genuinely separate, non-overlapping receivables (different legal entities on our side, disjoint invoice numbering, same external customer), so they're additive rather than one being consolidated into the other.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging to Treasury: I couldn't find any written intercompany/consolidation policy in docs or email (both systems came back empty in this environment), so the additive treatment rests on the entity and transaction evidence itself rather than a documented policy \u2014 solid, but worth a quick confirming glance if one exists elsewhere.",
